<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shillong</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shillong is a hill city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meghalaya">Meghalaya</a>. Founded as a British station in the 1860s on the lands of the Khasi state of Mylliem, it became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> in 1874 and remained so until Meghalaya was formed in 1972. Its cool climate and pine-covered hills earned it the name '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> of the East'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shillong.jpg" alt="Shillong" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prasanta Kr Dutta · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elephant_Falls_II,_Shillong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Shillong lies in the middle of the Shillong Plateau, the only major uplifted structure in the northern Indian shield, and is surrounded by hills. Three of these are revered in Khasi tradition: Lum Sohpetbneng, Lum Diengiei and Lum Shyllong. The name comes from Lei Shyllong, a powerful deity believed to live on Shillong Peak, which overlooks the city. The city is about 100 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a> by road, through hills and past Umiam Lake. Its climate is subtropical highland, with cool, very rainy summers and cool, dry winters. Its biggest market is Ïewduh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war">First Anglo-Burmese War</a> the British wanted a road connecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylhet">Sylhet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> across the Khasi and Jaintia Hills. David Scott, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> civil servant and agent of the governor-general for the North East Frontier, overcame opposition from the Khasi chiefs, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syiem">Syiems</a>, and in 1829 negotiated with the Syiem of Sohra for a sanatorium for the British. In early 1829 a Khasi confederation rose against British occupation, but by January 1833 its leader, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirot-sing">Tirot Sing</a>, had surrendered to Scott and was detained in Dacca. The British disliked the climate and facilities of Sohra, also known as Cherrapunjee, and moved to Shillong, which took its name because the new town lay below Shillong Peak.</p><p>The British designated Shillong the civil station of the Khasi and Jaintia Hills in 1864. In 1874 a separate Chief Commissionership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> was formed with its seat at Shillong, chosen for its convenient position between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> and Surma valleys and for its much cooler climate. It included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylhet">Sylhet</a>, the Naga Hills, the Lushai Hills and the Khasi, Jaintia and Garo Hills. A proclamation of 1878 made Shillong and its suburbs a station under the Bengal Municipal Act of 1876, with the villages of Mawkhar and Laban included by agreement with Haiñ Manik Syiem of Mylliem, dated 15 November 1878. An earthquake of 12 June 1897, of moment magnitude 8.1, killed 27 people in Shillong and destroyed much of the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Shillong was capital of undivided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> under British rule and for 25 years in independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. When the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meghalaya">Meghalaya</a> was created on 21 January 1972 it became the capital of Meghalaya, and Assam moved its capital to Dispur in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a>. It is the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-khasi-hills">East Khasi Hills</a> district. The rolling hills reminded the British of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, so they called it the Scotland of the East.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shillong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hima Khyrim emerged as one of the Khasi chiefdoms of the <b>Shillong</b> plateau; its origins are known from oral tradition, and the register's start date is approximate.</blockquote><a href="/state/khyrim-state">Khyrim State</a></li><li><blockquote>Hima Mylliem was one of the Khasi chiefdoms that emerged on the <b>Shillong</b> plateau; their origins are known from oral tradition, and the register's start date is approximate.</blockquote><a href="/state/mylliem-state">Mylliem State</a></li><li><blockquote>The British headquarters moved to <b>Shillong</b> in 1864.</blockquote><a href="/state/sohra-state">Sohra State</a></li></ul></section>
